<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Thakuri dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">879 CE – 1200 CE (approx.) · capital Kathmandu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The medieval rulers of the Kathmandu Valley between the Licchavis and the Mallas, an age of trade with Tibet and Buddhist manuscript copying.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/licchavi-kingdom-of-nepal">Licchavi Kingdom of Nepal</a> faded in the 8th century, the history of the valley is obscure. The Thakuri period is conventionally dated from 879, the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal-sambat">Nepal Sambat</a> era, though the founder is uncertain; later chronicles name Raghavadeva and credit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunakamadeva">Gunakamadeva</a> with founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kantipur</a>, the city that became Kathmandu.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Royal power was often divided, with rival kings ruling different towns of the valley at the same time. The valley prospered as the main route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a>, and its monasteries produced many illustrated Buddhist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, which became important sources as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> declined in India. Shaiva <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashupatinath-temple">Pashupatinath</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-buddhism">Newar Buddhism</a> flourished side by side, and Newari began to be written.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Thakuri rule ended around 1200 with the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ari-malla">Arimalla</a>, founder of the Malla dynasty, which ruled the valley until the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha</a> conquest of 1768 to 1769.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/thakuri-dynasty.jpg" alt="Thakuri dynasty" width="320" height="229"><figcaption>Image: Kratant maps · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_nepal.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>879 CE – 1200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kathmandu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shivadeva III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit, Newari</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Thakuri kingdom of Nepal, Nepal Thakuris</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1040&amp;focus=thakuri-dynasty" aria-label="Thakuri dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6919 1162 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Thakuri dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7239 1418L7239 1418M7239 1418L7242 1419"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7239 1418h0M7242 1419h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7239 1418h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7239 1418h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kathmandu. <a href="/atlas?year=1040&amp;focus=thakuri-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
